    "slot_number": "slot-7-1-dyn-5",
    "slot_key": "Real-time observation monitoring and adjustment",
    "slot_value": "During a typical session, the observing team should be able to see whether the telescope and instruments are behaving correctly, judge data quality quickly, and adjust the plan if conditions or results are not meeting expectations. If conditions change, the system should support quick recovery or adjustment so the night can stay productive. Real-time status and quality feedback is critical. Operators should be exposed to enough status to know what the system is doing, whether a subsystem is healthy, busy, delayed, or unavailable, and what part of the observing flow is affected. If a subsystem is unavailable, slow, or errors out, the interface should present a clear operational state and the next safe option, rather than raw technical noise. Low-level interaction details should stay hidden unless needed to diagnose a problem or approve a fallback action. For remote observing, feedback should feel near real-time for the operator to trust it, especially for motion, exposure state, and safety-related conditions; delays beyond a couple of seconds make good decisions harder and make it harder to tell whether a command actually took effect. Less critical status updates can tolerate more delay, but the core control loop should remain very responsive.",
